\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-1,0,1.$\n\n![](images/92/92a2dbaa5726e6a1ab83fa879ad7b630b05e68a8e366db214b74d85269a849a9.png)"}] ["q-50196" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097303 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b595fdc-ac24-53b1-b485-072a76711f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/83/83c166a9882661ec123b3f14bf76c9c7585052d0e85e24c4e41b7bbde4de9326.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330631} {:answer/id #uuid "71cb7ed8-c659-56d9-95c6-9bd73867660f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1e/1e746142e1dc26c247a6ac985c65072445658ad1f9b5ae298d246c1d81ab96bb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330973} {:answer/id #uuid "7a615d72-2639-5796-9876-984ecf702dec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/56/566935c224696f817a798a9e0f608ba384b5da6af1f0bfec6ad769e910f1230c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331033} {:answer/id #uuid "8feb98b8-4798-558b-9d2a-1181de6e3f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/4b/4bcb4f24caba3010cb8bfffecacbd392ad5951d4e42873d467c9007ad2b7ea46.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331227} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f5ab31-edff-5dee-88f0-7ee4a29422a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1f/1fd9c2f2358171f102969930fde05347f50bd497f725fdc4c24fec97490f15ad.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331457}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330631} :answer-field/id #uuid "f42fe6aa-d5ce-5802-8ca2-328c4a548b5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "27b042e7-c453-5f49-bac9-de5985bbacde" :question/math-academy-id "q-50196" :question/problem "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-3(x+1)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-3\\int (x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-3\\int (x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(x+1)^{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-1,0,1.$\n\n![](images/03/03a5bf5134dc7d551b2602145c4c25081a2ad274f02c95845e5789b288e618a1.png)"}] ["q-71053" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338886 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2615d5d0-577d-51e9-a7eb-7299c88c1240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336388} {:answer/id #uuid "2d4ee843-f5a5-5e6f-a7b4-d39ee29e9a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=-2{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336465} {:answer/id #uuid "935cffee-f030-54ce-8980-0ae960bd62a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=10-2{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337376} {:answer/id #uuid "b4aa127c-c35b-5236-91c3-effa79e34c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=10-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337635} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f526af-9e12-5d00-a48a-09643a747fff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337899}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336465} :answer-field/id #uuid "c72ca8b2-1291-5b13-840e-71b284db3d8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bd4ec376-93b4-54c8-9a98-7640cf3d729e" :question/math-academy-id "q-71053" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=10-2{e}^{x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $10$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nUsing the addition rule, then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(10-2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(10)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =0-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] ["q-8349" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88e6e851-77d9-50d0-b99d-b8ca4b35fe00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2xy=6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349690} {:answer/id #uuid "bd11a2c4-956c-5921-bf71-eef3b4403859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x=6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350126} {:answer/id #uuid "d27ff332-32be-52d1-8f0c-1c58dea87e89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy=6{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350296} {:answer/id #uuid "ee885d63-62c6-5299-8069-ab09eb83e825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{2}=6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350531} {:answer/id #uuid "fede89cf-752a-5d9b-a4c1-e5ef628a1c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+2xy=6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350690}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350296} :answer-field/id #uuid "e71f2d30-074a-5e70-868b-367fb755a8c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c6eeef64-0029-5193-805e-25131224aaa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-8349" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy=6{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nis already is written in standard form with  $P(x)=2x$ and $Q(x)=6{x}^{2}.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."}] ["q-8352" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d8dc75e-3cf3-5795-857d-b484a8bface7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348706} {:answer/id #uuid "43bb70a4-1ebc-568d-ac4e-2b15cf3ae1b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349062} {:answer/id #uuid "9c0edda0-970f-571f-af28-8b2bb516b055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349837} {:answer/id #uuid "aa32fc93-bb6e-5db9-9787-457ed8b63eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349959} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e4844b-3007-5cd6-b780-6e847081831e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{e}^{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350631}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349062} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf81e7df-70d6-59a2-9d91-cf73151d372e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ecc44493-c978-58e2-96eb-083d048c8f5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-8352" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x{y}^{-1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation cannot be written in standard form since the power of $y$ in the term $x{y}^{-1}$ is not $1.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs."}] ["q-8359" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088420 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d56ade3-8c2b-558b-8dee-d6c16408ae98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2x-x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348562} {:answer/id #uuid "19d75fc4-d565-5247-a3d2-0dd1d71481b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348663} {:answer/id #uuid "33f582cb-d125-5a7e-bc13-6fd636ea22e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348913} {:answer/id #uuid "a15aa42d-fc0c-537c-988d-61613c47dc74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x}y+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349879} {:answer/id #uuid "e907ba10-aa76-5dbf-bb32-8f9829e3aeb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350489}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350489} :answer-field/id #uuid "57ee4a69-5c37-5b5b-9d83-fd6eb1f2f193" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9fbd67f9-88e1-5554-8625-84ac1e940645" :question/math-academy-id "q-8359" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x-y}{x}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x}{x}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & +\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{2x}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & +{{\\frac{1}{x}}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y={{2}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)=2.$"}] ["q-8546" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a11d5a1d-e93d-5a5d-8111-e33d997e2841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(ye^{x}) = e^{x} \\cdot 2xe^{-x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087270} {:answer/id #uuid "e572ebc9-141f-578b-93ec-080a0deeb913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y \\cdot 2x) = 2x \\cdot 2xe^{-x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087271} {:answer/id #uuid "fd667700-3e8d-53d0-9c71-264753a1f8cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(yx^{2}) = x^{2} \\cdot 2xe^{-x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087272} {:answer/id #uuid "6e285ba0-0b5f-5e5a-a7bd-e5b3de551a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(ye^{x^{2}}) = e^{x^{2}} \\cdot 2xe^{-x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087273} {:answer/id #uuid "76e91a95-c637-5848-86e4-e944f3eee17e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(yx) = x \\cdot 2xe^{-x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087274}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9f8fe340-5770-5791-befc-e802db4415ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087269}] :question/id #uuid "8973fb88-5098-5e4b-a0b0-25638565dd93" :question/math-academy-id "q-8546" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)$:\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2xy = 2xe^{-x^{2}}$\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{d}{dx}(yI(x)) = I(x)Q(x)$, where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor."}] ["q-95984" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167441 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50433ce6-5f47-50dd-9e9b-85d17224a715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358441} {:answer/id #uuid "7227653e-d62c-513d-9a69-bdac1666e341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358748} {:answer/id #uuid "b29e28c8-4bf6-5d40-8c9d-8c40c5abc4ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359305} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c5eecb-38a5-5274-a5bd-d4d7a88d8192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359362} {:answer/id #uuid "dbea906c-0509-5763-971e-c278d2b7e4c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359679}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359305} :answer-field/id #uuid "c723b3f2-2a27-58ff-a975-f834b8df18f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3e9b8880-609b-5a27-b50e-083098d2cbea" :question/math-academy-id "q-95984" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{y}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=0$\n- $2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy=\\sqrt{x}$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)={e}^{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=3y.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}(xy+\\sqrt{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}(xy+\\sqrt{x}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] ["q-96663" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360692 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a7c8253-2398-5844-ab87-9f569178c088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358387} {:answer/id #uuid "604bb718-8188-5706-9d5e-bc5e825254c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358594} {:answer/id #uuid "64729ba5-db61-5904-a7f3-3348bf688c82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358631} {:answer/id #uuid "95c21078-c3b5-5a29-a41b-fa3dd5c92b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359063} {:answer/id #uuid "9903b498-b87b-528c-9348-0e7f32f90a53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359096}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359063} :answer-field/id #uuid "4becacdb-3199-5e03-8693-24305826113a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "21e5af7d-c901-55dc-94b7-2ce8f4a4e5f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-96663" :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}-4{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2x?$" :question/worked-solution "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}},$ which has order $4.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $4.$"}] ["q-96676" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234490 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "20db6c27-e8a9-50d0-bb54-61baf97c40e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-96676" :question/problem "Which of the following are first-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - $3{y}^{''}+2x{y}^{'}={x}^{2}y$\nII. - $5{x}^{2}y+3{y}^{5}{y}^{'}=4x$\nIII. - $\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{y}^{'}}+7{x}^{3}={e}^{y}$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is of first-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{''},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\n- Equation III is also a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] ["q-96711" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360698 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54cf01e5-ceba-59ae-b9ce-961e6fa63f1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358485} {:answer/id #uuid "6642d55d-578c-5eb5-acb5-736fb593558e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358645} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab571c7-f4b9-5fc6-b56d-7b725d4ac0e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358684} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a9b1d7-4195-562c-9576-a87690eb06cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359610} {:answer/id #uuid "e52cfd81-732f-5366-8b59-942dce48aad9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359782}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359782} :answer-field/id #uuid "418c151a-65ae-536f-b50c-e43314ab52fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5388ae42-2792-5587-8853-f61a559c11a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-96711" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})$\n- ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}=1$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}y+{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{e}^{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}+1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] ["q-96986" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0497b675-c4d7-5aec-bf1c-5d24dee89d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357794} {:answer/id #uuid "13713ac4-10e1-59ec-a786-cc04cf0b96f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357917} {:answer/id #uuid "23faf99c-a7e1-552f-8309-c422f0cf6b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358072} {:answer/id #uuid "732f6afd-9270-562c-a749-43bd8024a8e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358753} {:answer/id #uuid "78ed70b7-8f6a-5447-93db-c6e70c7ee75f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358800}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358800} :answer-field/id #uuid "da428f89-b735-5f26-9767-c7c21805d046" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ed0b0a68-7ad2-55d7-8d80-82f9a5c1ced6" :question/math-academy-id "q-96986" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}=3{y}^{′},\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1$\n- $2{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=2$\n- $4{y}^{′}-\\frac{1}{3}xy=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}=3{y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $2{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=2.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
